# Optional: your OWN values for some of the 30 TokenFactory secrets. Copy this file to `secrets.local.env` (same folder as config.env; it is git-ignored),
# keep only the lines you want, and run the deployment. The `secrets` phase writes these values to Vault; every key you do not list stays random.
# Used ONLY on the first run of the `secrets` phase (when secret/tokenfactory does not exist yet). To change a value later: VAULT-SECRETS.md section 4.
# Format: KEY=VALUE, one per line, no spaces around "=", no quotes needed. Never commit the real file.
#
# smtp-password=<the Resend API key>                     (or set SMTP_PASSWORD in config.env)
# kc-admin-password=<min 8 chars: upper, lower, digit, special>
# platform-admin-password=<min 8 chars: upper, lower, digit, special>      the admin@<DOMAIN> login of the platform
# platform-init-admin-password=<min 8 chars: upper, lower, digit, special>
# kc-admin-username=admin
# redis-password=<password of the Etisalat in-cluster Redis (sentinel); not the Xailon Azure Redis key>
# encryption-key=<openssl rand -base64 32>               DO NOT set this unless you must: it can never be rotated afterwards
# Other allowed keys (normally random, leave them out): db-password jwt-secret portal-jwt-secret admin-api-key ce-userinfo-signing-key
#   auth-state-signing-key kc-db-username kc-db-password openfga-db-password s3-access-key s3-secret-key stripe-webhook-secret stripe-secret-key
# All 30 keys (the live xailon-tf-prod set + jwt-secret, kc-db-username, openfga-db-password): cdd-email-hmac-key redis-password google-client-secret
#   microsoft-client-secret persona-api-key persona-webhook-secret zendesk-oauth-client-id zendesk-oauth-client-secret zendesk-signing-secret
#   cloudflare-tunnel-token random-test-secret (plus the ones above). Keys you leave out: random (internal) or empty (external integrations).
